Publish site to GitHub Pages via /docs

GitHub Pages can only serve from the repository root or /docs, and the Pages
setting for this repo was already pointed at /docs -- a folder that did not exist.
That is why the site was blank: GitHub had nothing to serve.

- Adds docs/index.html (the self-contained build) and docs/.nojekyll, which stops
  GitHub running Jekyll over the output.
- site/build.mjs now writes BOTH site/dist/index.html and docs/index.html in one
  step, so the published copy cannot drift from the source. A separate manual copy
  is a step people forget, and a stale published copy means Pages keeps serving an
  old page while the source looks correct -- which is exactly how dist/ went stale
  before this change.
- site/build.mjs now verifies its own rewrites. String.replace() does not throw when
  a pattern is missing, so an edit to site/index.html that moved one of the anchors
  would previously have silently produced a page that still called fetch() and
  therefore showed "Could not load data/findings.json" from file:// or Pages. The
  build now fails loudly instead. Verified by deliberately breaking the anchor: the
  build exits 1 with a clear message.
- docs/README.md documents that docs/ is generated output, not source.

Also includes the regenerated site data and the first six conclusion posts from the
Scribe agent, and the rebuilt page carrying the new Posts section.

Site data now reflects all six runs (80 turns, 2,592 tool calls, 209 artifacts)
rather than only the three phase* runs.
